     1  # Where should the record's outside anchor live? (item 3, experiment 1)
     2  
     3  An anchor has to show that a record's digest existed by a given time, in
a way the owner can't quietly backdate, edit or delete. This compares the 
candidates on that, measuring wherever possible. Run 2026-11-05 and checked by 
an independent reviewer agent; its corrections are folded in below.
     4  
     5  **Method.**
     6  - **Digest.** Every route anchors the same digest: the sha256 of 
`runs/count-2026-11-05T073638Z-0feb5e325c25.json`, which is `fe8d93a9…1de8`.
     7  - **CI runs.** `try_anchors.sh` and `.github/workflows/anchor-test.yml` 
ran on the `anchor-test` branch, three times on two runners each (x86-64 and 
arm64). Results are on the `ci-runs` branch under `anchors/`.
     8  - **Workspace checks.** Network reach, GH Archive, Software Heritage, 
offline verification of the CI tokens, and the public Rekor entries were checked
from this workspace.
     9  - **Caveat.** The first two runs' `attempts.jsonl` have invalid JSON 
numbers (`.22` with no leading zero, from `bc`). The values are readable, and 
the script is now fixed.
    10  
    11  ## Measured
    12  | Route | Result |
    13  |---|---|
    14  | RFC 3161, 6 free TSAs | granted 4/4 each, verified |
    15  | RFC 3161, DFN | granted 4/4, verify failed |
    16  | RFC 3161, Apple | no connection 4/4 |
    17  | OpenTimestamps | submitted 4/4, pending |
    18  | Sigstore attestation | ok in every run with it, in Rekor |
    19  | Gist via CI token | HTTP 403, 4/4 |
    20  | GH Archive | none of our events |
    21  | Software Heritage | repo not archived |
    22  
    23  **Details:**
    24  - **RFC 3161.** FreeTSA, DigiCert, Sectigo, SSL.com, GlobalSign and 
Certum all verified.
    25    - Each answered in 0.05–0.48 s with a 4.6–7.7 KB token, and each 
token's time matched the runner clock to within 1 s.
    26    - The DFN failure's cause wasn't captured; the script keeps only the 
last line.
    27    - FreeTSA's CA was downloaded from freetsa.org at run time, so it's 
trusted on first use, not an independent root. The other five chain to the 
system CA store.
    28    - **Offline re-check:** a DigiCert token re-verified in this 
workspace, and failed once one bit of the digest was flipped.
    29    - **After certificate expiry:** with `-attime` set to 2040, DigiCert's
token fails ("certificate has expired"; its TSA cert ends 2037-11-04). Verifying
at the token's own time passes, but that assumes the key wasn't compromised 
later. Long-term validity needs re-stamping before expiry, as in RFC 4998 
evidence records.
    30  - **OpenTimestamps.** Stamping took 1.7–5.7 s and gave a 0.7–0.9 KB 
proof. It's pending until a Bitcoin block confirms it. **Update 2026-11-06:** 
the local proof completed, with all four calendars attested in blocks 974650, 
974651, 974654 and 974681. `ots_check.py` replays each path to the block 
header's merkle root and matched 8 of 8 against headers from two explorers, 
blockstream.info and mempool.space. The earliest attestation is block time 
09:02:44Z, 32 minutes after stamping. A one-byte change to the file fails. The 
proof is committed here as `sample_record.json.ots`.
    31    - The official client verifies against a Bitcoin node, though a pruned
node is enough; in principle block headers alone are.
    32    - The opentimestamps.org web verifier works from a phone, at the cost 
of trusting that page.
    33    - It works from this workspace too.
    34  - **Sigstore.** `actions/attest@v2` signed the digest with the 
workflow's identity.
    35    - **Corrected after verifier round 1:** Rekor's v1 index (`POST 
/api/v1/index/retrieve`) finds the entries both by digest 
(`{"hash":"sha256:<D>"}`) and by workflow identity (`{"subject":"<SAN>"}`). Both
returned 6 on 2026-11-06, one per attesting job across 3 runs. The earlier "4" 
predates the third run. Entry 3692913518 has integratedTime 
2026-11-05T08:32:34Z.
    36    - The certificate names the repo, 
`anchor-test.yml@refs/heads/anchor-test`, commit `4ea7f2c…` and run 41995230106,
and is valid for 10 minutes.
    37    - **Verified without GitHub** by the reviewer, with sigstore-python 
4.5.1, both online and with `--offline`:
    38      `python3 -m sigstore verify github --bundle b.sigstore.json 
--cert-identity 
"https://github.com/5kasgard/exagium/.github/workflows/anchor-test.yml@refs/head
s/anchor-test" --repository 5kasgard/exagium --ref refs/heads/anchor-test --sha 
<commit> --trigger push sha256:<digest>`.
    39      A flipped digest or a wrong repository fails.
    40  - **Gist via CI token.** Refused with "Resource not accessible by 
integration", so a gist needs a personal token stored as a CI secret, or a 
manual step.
    41  - **GH Archive.** It now holds about 4–15 k events an hour, a small 
sample of GitHub. None of our pushes appear in the 21 hours scanned, including 
07:40–07:59 when we pushed; hour 8 wasn't published yet. Not a witness.
    42  - **Software Heritage.** The repo isn't archived. "Save code now" is 
possible but untested.
    43  - **GitHub repo activity.** `/repos/…/activity` returns GitHub's 
server-side push times without auth, including force pushes. Commit dates, by 
contrast, are set by the committer.
    44  - **Cost:** every route here is free.
    45  
    46  ## What each route can and can't prove
    47  | Route | Backdate | Delete or hide |
    48  |---|---|---|
    49  | Git commit/tag | owner (commit dates) | owner |
    50  | Gist | owner | owner |
    51  | RFC 3161 token | TSA only | owner (holds the token) |
    52  | OpenTimestamps | nobody practical | owner (holds the proof) |
    53  | Sigstore / Rekor | Sigstore only | detectable, not impossible |
    54  
    55  **Notes on the table:**
    56  - **Git.** A force push shows in the activity log, but deleting and 
recreating the repo wipes that log in one step. The activity log is GitHub's 
clock, so it's independent of the owner but not of GitHub.
    57  - **Gist.** Revisions can be force-pushed away, there's no activity log,
and CI can't write one.
    58  - **RFC 3161.** You trust one company's key per token. It needs 
re-stamping before the cert expires.
    59  - **OpenTimestamps.** It's slow, but there's no company to trust.
    60  - **Sigstore.** The owner can delete GitHub's copy of an attestation 
through the API; the Rekor copy stays. Removing a Rekor entry would show up 
through inclusion proofs and signed checkpoints, but only if someone checks. The
public-good instance has no SLA, and the move from Rekor v1 to v2 bears on how 
long it lasts. An attestation says "this workflow at this commit vouched for D 
at T", not that D's content is true.
    61  
    62  **Findings:**
    63  - **Repo plus gist gives no independence.** Both sit under one account 
and one custodian, and both can be rewritten by the same person. A gist is 
weaker than the repo: no activity log, and CI can't write to it.
    64  - **Existence isn't completeness, and a hash chain alone doesn't fix 
that.** Every route proves only that a digest existed. A chain where each record
includes the previous digest proves internal consistency, but the owner could 
still:
    65    - keep two forked chains and anchor both;
    66    - run privately and never append;
    67    - re-run until a good result comes up.
    68  
    69    **Corrected:** Rekor v1 *can* be searched by identity, which was wrong
here before. But the identity string includes the ref, so a search per ref 
misses runs on refs nobody thought to query. Rekor v2 has no search index at 
all, which leaves log monitoring (rekor-monitor) as the general answer.
    70  - **Mitigations to test:**
    71    - CI is the only writer.
    72    - Every run is anchored automatically, with a sequence number and the 
previous head.
    73    - The test plan is committed before the run (pre-registration).
    74    - Verifiers pin the workflow commit and ref.
    75    - An identity monitor watches Rekor for forks.
    76  
    77  ## Hypothesis for the next step (to test, not yet decided)
    78  - **The log lives in git:** hash-chained records, canonically encoded 
(RFC 8785 JCS, or hash the stored bytes) so any language can recompute them. CI 
is the only writer.
    79  - **Each chain head gets independent witnesses:**
    80    1. a Sigstore attestation from CI: public, identity-bound, verifiable 
without GitHub;
    81    2. RFC 3161 tokens from two commercial TSAs whose roots are in the 
system store: offline-verifiable, re-stamped before expiry;
    82    3. optionally, OpenTimestamps (no company to trust), and a periodic 
deposit outside GitHub (Software Heritage or Zenodo).
    83  
    84  **Still to measure:** whether `gh attestation verify` agrees with 
sigstore-python, and whether it accepts any workflow or branch in the repo when 
given only `-R`; and whether a monitor can see every anchor this workflow makes,
across all refs and on Rekor v2.
    85  
    86  ## Verifier round 1 (GLM-5.2 on Z, 2026-11-05): adjudication
    87  Full report and scripts: `verifier1/`. Each claim was re-checked here 
before being accepted.
    88  
    89  **Accepted:**
    90  - **Reproduction:** 64 RFC 3161 tokens and 6/6 Sigstore bundles verify 
offline under strict flags.
    91  - **DFN:** its root (DFN-Verein Community Root CA 2022) isn't in the 
store; the token itself is fine.
    92  - **RFC 3161 expiry:** every working TSA cert expires 2036–2042.
    93  - **Long-term validation:** no production RFC 4998 library for Python or
OpenSSL. So OTS is the long-term anchor, and RFC 3161 is short-horizon 
diversity.
    94  - **Equivocation:** R5 is closable for anchored runs, by monitoring, 
witness cosigning and archive snapshots.
    95  - **Completeness:** R4 can't come from any anchor. It needs 
pre-registration, mandatory deposit from CI, a reconciling monitor, and 
re-measurement as the backstop.
    96  - **Hashing:** hash the stored bytes, not a re-serialisation.
    97  - **Rekor search:** searching by identity works in v1, which corrects 
the claim above.
    98  
    99  **Rejected or corrected:**
   100  - **"Search by digest returns `[]`".** The request body was malformed 
(`{"queries":[…]}`). The documented form `{"hash":…}` returns 6.
   101  - **"A loose verifier (no `--ref`/`--trigger`) accepts another branch's 
attestation".** Z's own matrix contradicts this. With the full SAN including 
`@ref`, identity matching is exact (row 4), so another branch's certificate 
fails. The real risk is a verifier that checks only the repo, or matches the 
identity by regex or prefix.
   102  - **"Rekor v2 drops DSSE".** Unresolved, because sources disagree. It 
doesn't bind today: the public instance stays on v1 as default (Sigstore blog, 
"Rekor evolution"), with a year's notice before v1 freezes.
   103  - **"Apple's TSA works".** Unresolved. CI, with an open network, got no 
connection 4/4.


